title: Rivers, Coasts & Tectonics
subject: Geography
level: GCSE
emoji: 🌋
description: Physical geography – landforms and processes.
---
What is the source of a river? :: Where it begins, usually in upland areas.
What is a river's mouth? :: Where the river meets the sea.
What is a tributary? :: A smaller river that joins a larger one.
What is a confluence? :: The point where two rivers meet.
What is a watershed? :: The boundary of high land separating one drainage basin from another.
Name the four processes of river erosion. :: Hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, solution.
What is a waterfall formed by? :: A band of hard rock overlying softer rock that erodes faster, forming a plunge pool.
How is a meander formed? :: Erosion on the outer bank and deposition on the inner bank of a bend.
What is an oxbow lake? :: A meander cut off when the river breaks through the neck of a bend.
What is a floodplain? :: Flat land beside a river that floods when the river overflows.
What is longshore drift? :: Zigzag movement of sediment along a coast by waves hitting at an angle.
How is a wave-cut platform formed? :: A cliff is undercut and collapses, leaving a flat rocky platform.
How does a headland form? :: Hard rock is left sticking out as softer rock erodes into bays.
Order: cave → ? → stack → stump. :: Cave → arch → stack → stump.
What is a spit? :: A narrow ridge of sand or shingle joined to land at one end, formed by deposition.
What are the layers of the Earth? :: Inner core, outer core, mantle and crust.
What is a constructive plate margin? :: Two plates move apart; magma rises to form new crust.
What is a destructive plate margin? :: An oceanic plate subducts beneath a continental plate, forming volcanoes and trenches.
What is a conservative plate margin? :: Plates slide past each other, causing earthquakes (e.g. San Andreas Fault).
What scale measures earthquake magnitude? :: The Richter (or moment magnitude) scale.
What is the focus of an earthquake? :: The point underground where the earthquake starts; the epicentre is directly above.
What is the Ring of Fire? :: A belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific Ocean.
